+Tablet-sync and review-export are not yet designed. They consume the revision and review-state contract defined here; they do not define their own concurrency semantics.
 
 ## Architecture
 
-The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, submission-history retrieval, and review-outcome operations. Persistence keeps original submissions, their authors, and server receipt times. A submission carries an idempotency key so a connection failure does not create another edit on retry.
+The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, revision-history retrieval, and review-resolution operations. Persistence keeps original submitted revisions, their authors, and server receipt times. A submission carries an idempotency key so a connection failure does not create another edit on retry.
 
 A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+Each submitted revision names the visit ID and the base revision ID from which the worker edited. Its scientific fields are a complete visit payload, not field-level changes. The original payload and its base revision ID are immutable after acceptance.
 
-## Submission and Retrieval
+## Revision State and Submission
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a revision. Validate that the named base revision belongs to the named visit. Preserve the submitted payload separately from any later chosen current payload. A retry with the same key and identical submission returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different submission is rejected.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+When the base revision is the visit's current revision and the visit has no unresolved review work, the accepted submission becomes the current revision. When the base revision is not the current revision, or the visit already has unresolved review work, accept the submission as a competing revision and mark the visit as requiring review. The server does not merge fields from competing revisions.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Retrieval and Review Resolution
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+Retrieval returns the visit's current revision and whether the visit requires review. Revision history returns every submitted revision for the visit, including its base revision ID, author ID, receipt time, and complete payload. A current revision remains available while review is pending so tablets can retain a stable base, but it is not an export-ready outcome.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+Only a coordinator may resolve a visit requiring review. A resolution selects one retained complete revision as the next current revision and records the coordinator identity, resolution time, and the IDs of all revisions considered. The selected revision becomes the base revision for subsequent edits, and the visit no longer requires review. Resolution does not alter or delete any submitted revision.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Downstream Contracts
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+Tablet-sync must retain the base revision ID for each editable visit and submit complete revisions. It must handle an accepted competing revision without overwriting the local submitted payload.
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+Review-export must compare retained complete revisions for visits requiring review and must exclude those visits from the reviewed CSV until a coordinator resolves them. It may use the current revision only after the visit no longer requires review.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions with coordinator selection | Field-aware three-way merge | A complete submitted visit preserves the scientific context a worker supplied. Requiring review for every stale or pending-base submission creates a larger queue, but avoids merge rules that could combine scientifically related edits without recognizing their relationship. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. Concurrent edits are retained as competing whole-visit revisions rather than automatically merged.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Tablet-sync will define how a tablet presents a competing-revision acceptance and retains local retry state.
+2. Review-export will define the coordinator comparison interface and the reviewed CSV's treatment of unresolved visits.
